\details{
There are two primary types of monotonicity:
\itemize{
\item Weak, allowing the function to be increasing or constant
\item Strict, forcing the function to always increase and never stand still.
}

While we know that, in practice, transit vehicles rarely move backwards
(i.e., reverse) along their route, random noise in GPS observations will
introduce non-monotonicities (i.e., drifting backwards). Weak monotonicity
most accurately describes real transit vehicle trajectories:
we expect the vehicle to either move forwards, or stand still at a stop.
However, strict monotonicity is a convenient mathematical property that
allows us to find the inverse trajectory (i.e., retrieve time as a
function of distance).

There are three ways in which monotonicity can be enforced upon a function's
position:
\itemize{
\item Forward-stepping: Beginning at the beginning of a trip, iterate forwards
in time through distance observations. If the distance ever drifts backward,
it will be "pulled up" to the cumulative maximum distance observed by that
point.
\item Backward-stepping: Beginning at the end of a trip, iterate backwards in
time through distance observations. If the distance ever drifts forward,
it will be "pushed down" to the cumulative minimum distance observed
by that point.
\item Bidirectional: Because noise in the distance observations occurs
randomly in both directions, during stationary periods, forward-stepping
will bias the trajectory positively (i.e., the trajectory is ahead of the
actual vehicle position), and backward-stepping will bias the
trajectory negatively (i.e., the trajectory is behind the actual vehicle
position). Additionally, forward-stepping can disrupt the
acceleration cycle at the end of a stationary period, and backward-stepping
can disrupt the deceleration cycle at the beginning of a stationary
period. The bidirectional approach remedies this by performing both
directions of correction on the original data, then averaging the two
outcomes.
}

Choose between the two monotonicity types by setting
\code{add_distance_error}. If \code{add_distance_error = 0} (the default), a weakly
monotonic trajectory is returned; otherwise, the trajectory will be
strictly monotonic, with a perturbation of \code{add_distance_error} added (for
forward-stepping) or subtracted (for backward-stepping) in flat regions.
Choose between the two step directions using \code{direction}.

In addition to distance corrections, some applications (e.g., fitting a
velocity-informed interpolation spline) require speeds to satisfy certain
monotonic conditions. If \code{correct_speed = TRUE}, the following corrections
will be made:
\itemize{
\item For strict monotonicity (\code{add_distance_error > 0}), speeds must be
non-zero. At each point where the recorded \code{speed == 0}, the speed will be
replaced by \code{add_distance_error} divided by the time between that point and
the previous point.
\item For both strict and weak monotonicity, speeds will be adjusted to meet the
Fritsch-Carlson (1980) constraints. Often, only a handful of input
\code{speed} values will be adjusted.
}

If recorded speed values are not present, set \code{correct_speed = FALSE}.
However, if you are interested in later fitting a velocity-informed
interpolating curve, such as Fritsch-Carlson's piecewise cubic polynomials,
consider setting \code{correct_speed = TRUE} to guarantee a monotonic
interpolating curve.

After using this function to perform corrections, use
\code{validate_monotonicity()} to check if weak, strict, and Fritsch-Carlson
speed conditions are met.
